## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code forcibly aborts a thread after a timeout. In .NET 8, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006). The replacement is `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` with cooperative cancellation.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Block until completion or timeout
            task.Wait(cts.Token);
            return task.Result;
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerException is not null)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException);
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async version
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);
        var completed = await Task.WhenAny(task, Task.Delay(timeout));

        if (completed != task)
        {
            cts.Cancel();
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }

        return await task; // Unwraps exceptions naturally
    }
}
```

### If `work` is long-running, accept a token

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    try
    {
        return Task.Run(() => work(cts.Token), cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Mitigation |
|---------|------------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cooperative cancellation only works at checkpoints. If `work` is a tight CPU loop that never checks the token, it won't stop. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Always `using` it — leaks timers otherwise. |
| **`Task.Run` token only prevents scheduling** | The token passed to `Task.Run` prevents the task from *starting* if already cancelled. The work itself must also check `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()`. |
| **Uncooperative code (can't modify `work`)** | If you truly can't make `work` check a token, the only safe option is `Process.Kill` on a child process. There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort`. |

### What Was Removed

| Old API | Replacement |
|---------|-------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` / timeout via constructor |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed entirely — not needed |
| `new Thread(...)` + `thread.Start()` | `Task.Run(...)` |

### Fundamental Difference

**.NET Framework:** The runtime *forcibly injects* `ThreadAbortException` into the target thread at any point.

**.NET 8:** Cancellation is *cooperative* — the work must voluntarily check `CancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested` or call `ThrowIfCancellationRequested()`. If it doesn't, it keeps running until completion regardless of cancellation requests.
